\title{Why the subspace constant is sharp}
\taxon{Proposition}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Remark 2.2; reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026.}

\p{\strong{Statement.} The constant in [the subspace inequality](cn-0024) cannot be decreased.}

\p{\strong{Proof.} Write #{W=P\oplus Q} and divide the #{m} indices into groups of sizes #{a=\lfloor m/2\rfloor} and #{b=\lceil m/2\rceil}. In the first group set #{(U_i,V_i)=(P,Q)}; in the second set #{(U_i,V_i)=(Q,P)}. Every [designated pair](cn-0076) is disjoint. Only indices from different groups contribute, with total}

##{ab\dim P+ab\dim Q=\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor\dim W.}

\p{For example, three indices split #{1+2} give total #{2\dim W}. This construction makes the balanced product #{e(m-e)} in the proof less mysterious.}
